A||N||12||TRL46202||Should I keep it????||||1||
Z||000000||TRL46202||01-26-2000||04:35 PM||||Thinking of selling my 87GT (Auto/156,000) I would be asking $3,500.00 It has been hit in the rear and the back asorb was replaced with 2X6 wood bolted to the frame but the rear skin wasn't damaged at all except for some scratches. the car had been repainted and kind of dull when I got it. I buffed the paint and tuned it up and repaced all fluids and it runs tops. The interior is in great shape no rips/tears/cracks (seats look new,they had seat covers on them since new) has new tires and brakes and runs like a dream. Am I crazy or what??? is $3,500. to cheap, I've had alot of people wanting it, is an 87 a collectors or will it be one $$$$$ ???<P>thanks!||||reg||1
Z||000001||TRL46202||01-26-2000||04:51 PM||||Added for the above: I can use the money to finish some work on the house and besides I will be looking in the future for an 88GT. Will they go up that much in the near future??<P>Thanks Again!||||reg||1
Z||000002||four50four||01-26-2000||06:23 PM||||sorry man, but that seems like a lot of money for a fairly high-milage, damaged, non-rare fiero. just my opinion however||||reg||1
Z||000003||DRH||01-26-2000||07:01 PM||||I'd have to agree with 454. Sounds a little high. Prices do vary by area though. <P>Check out the link below for a Fiero price guide. This is by no means a final authority but should give you some idea. Yours in fair condition would be 3200 but it shows a 500 deduct for 130,000 miles (as high as it goes). You mentioned an 88 GT... note that they are going for almost 2000 more than a comparable 87.<P> <BR> <A HREF="http://home.xnet.com/~paulv/Price%20Guide.htm" TARGET=_blank>http://home.xnet.com/~paulv/Price%20Guide.htm</A> ||||reg||1
Z||000004||Cooter||01-26-2000||08:04 PM||||According to that link, my 88 gt is worth about $4500. WOW, not bad for a $2000 (so far) investment. I am sorry, but I just dont think that an "average" 88 GT is worth that much. I used to have a link that gave a better estimate as to the value. Try searching autotrader.com and see what they are selling for there. That would give you a good idea what to get for yours.<P>Found it: <A HREF="http://www.fiero.net/price.html" TARGET=_blank>http://www.fiero.net/price.html</A> <p>[This message has been edited by Cooter (edited 01-26-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000005||Tom88gt||01-27-2000||07:58 AM||||If the previous owner didn't replace the energy absorber and just used wood then what else did he skip?  The energy absorber apparently did it's job.  But if the car is hit from the rear again the damage will be more severe.  Wood is fairly incompressable.  <P>I think $3500 is too much.  Check the classifieds and see what comparable (according to sellers' descriptions) Fieros go for.  <P>The only advantage to the '88s is the suspension.  But I've heard that a modified 84-87 can better a stock '88.  But I've never compared the two side by side.||||reg||9
Z||000006||DRH||01-27-2000||11:17 AM||||Hey Cooter,<P>Interesting side notes:<P>- When you get a price on fiero.net there is a note: "Thanks to Paul Vargyas for an outline of general Fiero prices." The original site I posted is HIS site. Makes me think fiero.net just did a front end to Paul's prices. <P>- Paul's site was recently updated and to the best of my recollection the 88 GT went up about $1000. The fiero.net site does not say when it was last updated. <P>- One of the links from Paul's price page explains that prices can vary greatly by condition and where you are. Having spent the first 26 years of my life in Alabama, I'm guessing it would be on the lower end of the range.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"> Still sounds like you got a great deal on your car though!!!<P>||||reg||1
Z||000007||TRL46202||01-27-2000||11:38 AM||||Thanks guys! I picked the car up for $2,000. Like I said it looks and runs great. But since I know it's been wreaked (no visable damage, see above)it kind of leaves a stail note. I don't need the money and it dosn't hurt to keep it, I just want to know if this one will go up in value or should I bother with it?????<BR>PS; It's loaded (sunshade included and new tires!)No Leaks!!!!<P>Thanks again!!!<BR>Thanks! ||||reg||1
Z||000008||Tom88gt||01-27-2000||01:47 PM||||Well, as far as appreciation.  I don't think the car has much potential outside the relatively small kit car industry and a few die hard fans who will pay a few $ extra for a clean one.  When the Fiero was cancelled, there were not too many modern alternatives.  Now there are several small 2 seaters on the market.<P>I say the 88s are probably worth a premium equal to the cost of a full aftermarket suspension upgrade to an 84-87 (springs, shocks, swaybars, brakes).  But you can probably buy an 88 4cyl parts car for next to nothing and swap it all over.  <P>But I'm no expert.  Research the market and see what's out there.||||reg||1
Z||000009||Cooter||01-27-2000||04:24 PM||||DRH,<BR>I hope I did not offend you with my post. It was my intention just to have a different price range for comparison. It seems that the Fiero.net prices are more close to what I see here in Alabama, but other places will be different. Sorry if anyone misunderstood what I meant to do.||||reg||1
Z||000010||DRH||01-27-2000||05:12 PM||||Cooter,<BR>No offense taken. I had seen the site you posted before but lost it in all my links.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"> I just happened to notice the "thanks to" note while looking at it and thought that was pretty interesting.  <P>You have it made in Alabama... better prices, Ed Parks, AND you can put things in your trunk without it falling thru the rust! Hmmm... maybe I should move back!  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"><P>Later, David.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000011||Tom88gt||01-28-2000||02:47 PM||||TRL, same here.  Hope I didn't sound too curt with you about the value of your 87GT.  I'm just trying to be up front and honest with you.  <P>Personally, I wish Fieros were hot items.  But one of the main reasons I got mine was that they are undervalued (You get a great car for the money).  And unfortunately, are probably going to stay that way.||||reg||7
Z||000012||RZURCTN||01-28-2000||03:02 PM||||I have to agree with Tom88gt.  But I love the fact that Fiero's are "undervalued".  I picked mine up for $500 US and it certainly fits my budget!  So far my total investment is up to $650, that bought a parts car and another engine.  Now evertyhing is running great and I can enjoy the fruits of my labors.  So what if there isn't a market for Fieros', we all know the sentimental value of our respective machines.  That's really all that matters to me.  <BR>   Maybe in the year 2004, when my car is 20 years old, it will be worth something as a "classic".  I doubt it, but you never know!  So I agree that you probably won't get the amount you want for your 87GT, but maybe you'll find someone who really wants it bad!!!!   Good Luck!  If I had the money, and my wife would let me ;0, I'd be running a "Half-way House" for Fieros!!!||||reg||1
